  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 AM MST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nPrecipitation should end this morning and the winds should \nweaken considerably this afternoon with clearing skies expected.\nLooks like we will have dry weather into Sunday. A shortwave \ntrough arriving from the west/southwest will bring another round\nof precipitation Sunday night into Monday. Precipitation \namounts with that system should range from a few hundredths to \n0.25 inches for the Snake River Plain and Magic Valley. The \neastern highlands and central mountains should see 0.25 to 0.50 \ninches of liquid while areas around Stanley will see 0.50 to \n0.75 with over an inch likely along the Sawtooths. This is \nanother warm system with most snow accumulating above 5500 feet \nwith 1 to 3 inches likely. Higher slopes around the Sawtooths \ncould see more than 6 inches. We will see a brief break of only \na few hours before precipitation tries to work back into the \nsouthern highlands Monday night into Tuesday. But this is a \nsplit-flow system with the track of the low moving through the \nfour corners region. So we will probably not see much of \nprecipitation with that system, if any, assuming that track \nholds. Temperatures will remain slightly above normal through \nTuesday and then cool back towards normal.",
